       "24x24": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/6089a55c52a3d400690692f7/7a62c587-b0f1-4cd4-982c-21b3837dc64a/24",
       "32x32": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/6089a55c52a3d400690692f7/7a62c587-b0f1-4cd4-982c-21b3837dc64a/32",
       "48x48": "https://avatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net/6089a55c52a3d400690692f7/7a62c587-b0f1-4cd4-982c-21b3837dc64a/48"
      },
      "displayName": "Ralf Claussnitzer",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=6089a55c52a3d400690692f7",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": "<p>I was not so much thinking about RDF in particular but that could be a one side of the same problem. What I was actually thinking about is invalid XML documents (my use case mostly). Or more general: <em>POSTed content that does violate the specified request content type</em>. For example sending a GIF with content type application/jpeg. Currently there is no way to protect Fedora from this. </p>\n\n<p>In the end I will need to implement an extra-Fedora HTTP endpoint to check the transmitted files against very specific content types rules. For example, we use a custom content type application/vnd.qucosa.mets+xml which defines certain structural constraints.</p>\n\n<p>Since this is very application specific it should not be included in Fedora, I agree. I'm actually not sure  if API-X is the appropriate solution for this. There is Camel now and it can produce HTTP endpoints for integrating with Fedora.</p>",
     "created": "23/Feb/21 2:38 AM",
